# Line endings for the scripts users run directly.
#
# Every .bat / .ps1 blob in this repo was stored LF-only, so whether a fresh
# clone produced a working installer depended entirely on the cloner's
# core.autocrlf. On Windows that default is usually `true` and it worked by
# luck; with autocrlf=false, or via GitHub's "Download raw file" button, the
# user gets an LF-only batch file. cmd.exe tolerates LF for simple lines but
# not reliably for the multi-line parenthesised blocks and goto targets these
# installers are built from, and the failure is a syntax error mid-install
# rather than a clean refusal.
#
# Pinning the ending in the repo makes the shipped artefact deterministic
# instead of dependent on the reader's configuration.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# The POSIX side is the mirror case: a CR would reach the shebang line and
# produce "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M".
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Binary assets, marked explicitly rather than left to git's NUL-byte sniffing.
#
# Every bundled web font shipped corrupt for the site's whole existence: each
# .woff2 was ~80% larger than the size in its own header, Chromium refused all
# twelve with "OTS parsing error", and the site quietly fell back to Georgia for
# anyone who did not happen to have Cinzel installed locally. That is invisible
# on a machine that does have them, which is how it survived.
#
# `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`: it takes these files out of eol
# conversion entirely, so no combination of core.autocrlf on any contributor's
# machine can rewrite a byte of them. Cheap insurance for a class of bug whose
# symptom appears nowhere near its cause.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
